# DESCRIPTION

MaliCore is what a program needs to drive a Mali G720 through the `csf` capset (mali(3)) without a compiler: it builds records and command streams as words and bytes, and the caller puts them in GPU memory.
This page is its images, blend and draws; the shader compiler's output and the FAU layout are in valhallbackend(2).

## Images and layouts

A `TexelFormat` is the 22-bit format word of valhall 05: the component order, the format index and the sRGB bit, with the texel's size (the bytes of a block, for a compressed format), the plane's clump format and the block's size in texels.
The table of the colour formats (the 8, 16 and 32-bit families, rgb10a2, rg11b10f, rgb9e5), the depth and stencil planes (Z16, Z32, Z24X8 and S8 in order GRBA), ETC2, EAC and BC blocks, and ASTC 2D blocks (a plane of type 4 with the block's codes and the decode-wide bit) is TexelFormats.swift; `CompressedFeatures` reads TEXTURE_FEATURES[0].
The formats are `rgba8`, `rgba8srgb`, `bgra8`, `bgra8srgb` (the same index with order BGRA, so the bytes in memory are B, G, R, A) and `r8` (order RGB1: it reads (R, 0, 0, 1)).
An `ImageLayout` is the memory of an image: the levels packed in order each 64-byte aligned, the layers a whole array stride apart, the plane rounded up to 4096 bytes; the elements of a compressed level are blocks, and a volume's level has a surface for each slice (the depth halved with the level).
The layout is `linear` (row-major, the row stride a multiple of 64) or `uInterleaved`: 16 by 16 texel tiles in row-major order, the texels of a tile in the order of the u-curve (the bits `y3, x3^y3, y2, x2^y2, y1, x1^y1, y0, x0^y0` from the most significant), the row stride the bytes from one row of tiles to the next; or `interleaved64K` (the 64 KiB layout, clump ordering 8, a render target's block format 8): tiles of 64 KiB of elements in row-major order (`tile64K`: 256 x 256 elements of 1 byte down to 64 x 64 of 16), the 16 x 16-element blocks of a tile stored in row-major order, the elements of a block in the u-curve's order (`offset64K`), the row stride the bytes of a row of tiles, and every level whole tiles.
`texelOffset` is the address arithmetic; `upload` and `download` copy a packed row-major array of texels into and out of the layout.
AFBC, AFRC, block-linear and YUV formats are not made.

## Descriptors

A `PlaneDescriptor` is 32 bytes for one level of one layer: the 48-bit address, the level's size, the row stride, the width and height and the clump ordering and format (a RAW clump of the texel's size).
`ImageLayout.planes` gives them in the order a texture's plane array holds them, the layer slowest and then the level.
A `TextureDescriptor` is 32 bytes: dimension, format, size, swizzle, texel interleave (set for every layout but linear, and for a block-compressed format), levels, the address of the plane array (32-byte aligned), the array size and depth.
A `BufferDescriptor` is 32 bytes: the type (9) and buffer type (simple, structure, tiler heap or vertex packet), the size (low half in word 1, high half in word 5), the 64-bit address and, for the structure type, the element size in word 4; a texel buffer is a structure whose word 7, `conversion`, is the 22-bit pixel format word of its format, which a shader reads and passes to the conversion loads and stores.
A `SamplerWords` is 32 bytes: wrap modes, minify and magnify filters, mipmap mode, level-of-detail limits and bias, normalised coordinates and the border colour.
Round to nearest even is cleared when both filters are nearest, so nearest sampling at a texel's centre is exact.
A descriptor refused by its field limits throws `DescriptorError`.

## The descriptor heap

A device has one bindless heap: a table of 32-byte descriptors, the textures and samplers by index, which is table 1 of the resource table (table 0 is the driver's).
`DescriptorHeap.add` appends a descriptor and returns its index; `handle(index:)` is the shader's name for it, `1 << 24 | index`.
The caller puts the heap's bytes in memory of its own, aligned to 32 bytes, and gives its address and size to a pass: the pass makes the resource table, a 64-byte block of four 16-byte entries whose entry 1 is the heap, and sets the fragment stage's `SRT` register to its address and the entry count.
The storage images are table 2 (`DescriptorHeap.storageTableNumber`), and so are the texel buffers: their descriptors follow the images', from slot 1024 for the uniform ones and 2048 for the storage ones, in the same table.

A shader names the heap's entries through FAU words that hold the handle of each binding's first element (`GraphicsFauLayout.imageBindings`), which the draw fills from its `images`.
`TextureHandle.wide` and `packed` are the two forms of the handle source of a texture instruction (valhall 12b.2.1); the compiler uses the wide one.

## Blend

`BlendDescriptor(state:)` is the fixed-function descriptor for one 8-bit unorm or sRGB colour target.
A state with blending off or the equation source ONE, destination ZERO is opaque (mode Opaque, equation replace); a partial write mask is fixed-function with the replace equation and Load Destination; an empty mask is off.
Otherwise the equation is rearranged to `A + B * C` by the seven rows of valhall 09.4.2 for the colour and alpha functions separately (`BlendEquationEncoder.encode`): `BlendEquation.alpha` (SRC_ALPHA and ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA for colour, ONE and ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA for alpha) is the function words `0x503` and `0xD32`, `BlendEquation.premultiplied` is `0xD32` in both.
The constant is one 16-bit scalar, `trunc(k * 65280)`, and must be the same in every channel the equation names.
Load Destination is set whenever the equation, the mask or a factor reads the destination.
What needs a blend shader (a logic operation, alpha to one, minimum and maximum, the saturated source alpha, a constant that differs between channels or from the first fixed-function target's) throws `BlendRefusal.needsBlendShader` with the reason; `BlendDescriptor.planned(_:renderTarget:isSRGB:firstConstant:)` is the whole decision of valhall 09.7.1: the fixed-function or opaque descriptor, or a descriptor in mode Shader (words `[load | enable | round, 0, 0, address]`) with the `BlendShaderSpec` the shader computes (the target, sRGB, blending and its equation, the write mask, a `LogicOperation`, alpha to one; its `opaqueOperand`, `conversionWord` and `pixelIndexWord` are the shader's operands).
A `PassDraw` gives the code of each blend shader by target (`blendShaders`) and the blend constants (`blendConstants`, words 0 to 3 of the fragment FAU, `GraphicsFauLayout.blendConstantWord`); `RenderPass.build` places each distinct shader, 64-byte aligned, in the pass's block (so it is in the fragment shader's 4 GiB window) and puts its low 32 bits in the descriptor.

## Draws

A `DrawPassRequest` is a render pass of `PassDraw`s: for each, its compiled vertex and fragment shaders, vertex count, push constants (the same words for both stages), blend descriptor and heap handles.
A `PassDraw`'s shaders, blends, state and bindings are one shared object, copied only when a draw that shares it changes one of them; `PassDraw(sharingState:vertexCount:instanceCount:push:)` makes a draw that shares another's, with counts and push constants of its own.
`RenderPass.build` lays the pass's records and streams in a block of GPU memory (the shared records, each distinct shader's program descriptor and code once, each draw's blend descriptor and FAU blocks) and runs the draws one after another in the one tiler pass, so a blended draw blends over the earlier ones in the tile buffer; a pass has any number of draws. Each run's registers are written where they differ from the run before it in the stream (a run leaves them as they were), and all of them after an indirect draw, whose counts the stream loaded.
A `StepChain` orders a step of a submission after the one before it on the GPU: given one to a `DrawPassRequest` (`chain`), a `ComputeDispatchRequest` or `TimestampStream.build`, each stream starts by waiting (SYNC_WAIT64, greater) for the 64-bit sync object at its address to pass `waitAbove`, then cleans and invalidates the LSC and invalidates the texture unit's and the tile readers' caches (FLUSH_CACHE2, the L2 left alone), and the step's last stream (a pass's fragment stream) ends, after its tail's clean, by adding one to it (SYNC_ADD64, system scope), so the next step's streams, on another queue of the group, start once it is done (`CSBuilder.waitForChain`, `signalChain`; registers 68 to 71, free at a stream's start, are its scratch).
A `PassDraw` may name index data (`IndexSource`: its address, the bytes from it, `IndexSize` 16 or 32 bits), a first index, a base vertex (the first vertex of a draw with no index data), a first instance, `IndirectArguments` (an address whose words the stream loads into the count registers when it runs; the draw's own counts are then unused) and the address of an occlusion counter (`occlusionQuery`, with the counter mode in the draw flags).
A `DrawPassRequest`'s `queryReports` are the addresses of the occlusion reports of its queries: the fragment queue stores zero over each and flushes the stores before its fragment work (`queryReadback` has it load the first back through the GPU after the tail's flush, for a probe).
A draw with `IndirectArguments` is branched over when its count of vertices or indices or of instances is zero, and an `indirectGrid` dispatch when any dimension is zero.
`probe` fields (`flags0Or`, `flags0Clear`) change bits of the draw's flags 0 after the recipe has made them.
A `DrawPassRequest` may have a `FramePreload`: a fragment shader the hardware runs for each tile before the draws (the framebuffer's first pre-frame mode is always, and its draw descriptor is the first of the three the parameters name), reading an image through a heap of its own and writing it as the colour; and `storageAddress` and `storageBytes`, the storage images' descriptors as table 2 of the pass's resource table (a `ComputeDispatchRequest` has `storageTable` for the same, and `indirectGrid` for a grid the stream loads from memory).
`TimestampStream` is the stream of timestamps: STORE_STATE of the system timestamp to each address, with every slot waited for before it and the caches cleaned after.
A `RenderPassRequest`, the pass of one draw, takes the same heap (`heapAddress`, `heapBytes`, `images`, `dummySampler`) and gives the fragment stage the root pointer as its first push constant, as it does the vertex stage.
A draw may kill an earlier fragment (valhall 06.10.3) only if it does not read the destination and cannot discard.
A draw whose fragment shader can discard sets the draw flags' shader-modifies-coverage bit (valhall 06.9.1).
The colour target is `rgba8`, `bgra8` or, for sRGB, `rgba8srgb` or `bgra8srgb` (`ColourFormat`): the same bytes, whose clear word is encoded (`ColourFormat.srgbEncode`, no libm) and whose blend descriptors carry the sRGB bits, so the blend runs in light; the render target descriptor's own sRGB bit is left clear.
The target is cleared at the start of the pass; a pass that loads the target from memory needs frame shaders, which are not made.

## Pipeline state

A `PassDraw` has a `DrawPipelineState`, whose default is the colour-only opaque triangle list: the primitive mode (`DrawMode`: points, lines, line strip, triangles, triangle strip, triangle fan, with the minimum vertices each makes), primitive restart (all ones of the index type), culling and winding (the draw flags' cull bits, set for the triangle modes only), the line width or point size in `PRIMITIVE_SIZE`, a viewport (`DrawViewport`: the vertex shader's window transform in its FAU block, and the viewport rectangle register, truncated, clamped to the target and ordered as valhall 06.7.2 has it) and a scissor, and a `ZSDescriptorState`.
A draw whose vertex shader writes ViewportIndex has all of its pipeline's `viewports` and `scissors`: its vertex FAU block holds the address of a `ViewportTable` (16 entries of 32 bytes, each viewport's scale and offset, which the shader reads by the index), and it is cut by the first scissor. `DrawPipelineState.scissorProbe` makes it a probe of the scissor array instead (valhall 06.2: the primitive flags' bit 21, and the scissor register the address and length of a `ScissorArray`, 256 boxes in the scissor box's format, box k viewport k's rectangle cut by scissor k, the viewport registers the rectangle and depth range around every viewport's). `ScissorArray.outside` is a box nothing of a target is in.
`ZSDescriptorState` is a depth/stencil descriptor with the test on (valhall 06.8): the depth function and write, the stencil functions, operations, masks and references of both faces, the bias factors and clamp, and depth clip or clamp.
A draw with one has a descriptor of its own.
A `DrawPassRequest` may have a `ZSTarget`: the depth format (`DepthFormat`: 16-bit, 24-bit in a 32-bit element or 32-bit float, written as D16, D24X8 or D32 and in the tile as D16, D24 or D32, valhall 08 sections 8.2.2 and 8.5.1), the planes the pass stores (linear, each with its address and strides; a plane not stored has no record, so no write), the clears the tile starts from, and a stencil plane (S8); the parameters block's word 12 and the 64-byte extension after it carry them, and the framebuffer group is 64 bytes longer.
Depth and stencil are loaded from an image only by a frame shader (valhall 08.2.1), which is not made: a pass clears them.
With a `ZSTarget` the draw's kill and update operations are decided by `PixelOperations.derive`, valhall 06.10.2's procedure for arch 12: a shader that writes depth, or that can change coverage while the state writes depth or stencil, updates late; a shader with memory side effects kills late; otherwise both are early.
A pass without one keeps the colour-only decision.
A shader that writes the depth takes the depth source from the shader (word 4's source field 3), whatever the state, so such a draw has a descriptor of its own.
An `ImageLayout` of kind `afbc` is AFBC (valhall 05 section 5.11): 16 x 16 superblocks with linear headers and the sparse body, a level's `rowStride` the header row stride, `headerBytes` the header area and `bodyOffset` where the body starts; its plane descriptor is the AFBC plane (03 section 3.9.6) and a render target or a depth plane of it is block format 12 with its compression mode and colour transform (08 sections 8.4.6 and 8.5.1), and reverse issue order and the clean tile write where the effective tile is smaller than a superblock.
A `DrawPassRequest` may have a `CRCTable` (`crc`): target 0's checksums for transaction elimination (08 section 8.6), in the depth/stencil/CRC extension with the empty tile's word (the clears' signature and the table's counter), and the framebuffer's CRC and empty tile read and write enables, used where the pass is of one layer and one sample with an effective tile of 256 pixels or more; and `sampleTable`, a sample-pattern table the caller keeps, in place of the pass's own.
A `DrawPassRequest` may have `extraTargets` (`ExtraColourTarget`: a format, an address and a clear colour), up to seven after the first, all of the first's size: the tile plan gives each its place, the framebuffer group has a render-target descriptor for each (and the tagged pointer counts them), and each draw's `extraBlends` are the blend descriptors of those targets, in an array whose positions are the targets (valhall 09.2.3).
The render-target mask and write mask of a draw's flags are the colour outputs its fragment shader writes; a target it doesn't write has an off descriptor.
A pass with several targets can't keep any target's contents.

